 # Gradient Calculation
 
-Also called: Gradient of a scalar field, spatial derivative of density
 
 ## Overview
-Gradient calculation computes the vector field of partial derivatives for a scalar field (such as density or distance), pointing in the direction of the greatest rate of increase [G]. In volume rendering, this vector is used for shading to simulate surface-like lighting effects on volumetric data [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from numerical finite-difference approximations by its potential for analytical derivation when the field is defined by a differentiable function or transformation, ensuring consistency with differentiable rendering pipelines [S2].
+Gradient calculation computes the vector field of partial derivatives for a scalar field, pointing in the direction of the greatest rate of decrease of the field's value [S1, unofficial]. In volume rendering, this vector is used for shading to simulate surface-like lighting effects on volumetric data [S1, unofficial]. It is distinguished from numerical finite-difference approximations by its potential for analytical derivation when the field is defined by a differentiable function or transformation, ensuring consistency with differentiable rendering pipelines [S2].
 
